From 0cf79c7117416f36fe6e34643a64d32a5546badc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:16:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(seo): head-term rail on 40 pages, /team, and the guide
 keyword layer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Executes the keyword-domination directive's Step 1, and the part of Tier 1
that is worth executing. Three things, in order of how much they matter.

1. CommercialHeadTermRail — the two money pages, forced into every crawl path

   /hardwood-flooring-toronto and /hardwood-floor-refinishing-toronto were
   reachable from the footer and from each other, and nowhere else. A footer
   link appears on every URL, so it distinguishes nothing, and every serious
   crawler discounts it for exactly that reason.

   The rail now renders in the body of the homepage, the services index, all
   32 service-area pages and all 11 guides — roughly 90 in-content links into
   two URLs, with descriptive anchor text, and on the service-area pages with
   the city interpolated into it. That is the mechanism by which a site tells
   a crawler which of its own pages it considers most important.

   Every figure is derived from lib/pricing.ts and lib/framework.ts, which is
   the only reason it is safe to render the same numbers in ninety places.
   It uses .shell plus its own .chr-* block rather than borrowing .clp-card or
   .tlx-card across the design-system boundary.

2. /team — who does the work, without inventing anyone

   Answers "who installs hardwood floors in Toronto" and, more importantly,
   "does this company use subcontractors" — which the site previously answered
   in one line inside /about's entity answers. Salaried employees, the same
   crew first board to final coat, and the lifetime workmanship warranty with
   its actual exclusions, all sourced to entity-answers.ts, BUSINESS_NAP and
   §4.1 of the contract document.

   The supplied draft named an individual and asserted crew tenure of "more
   than a decade". Neither is published anywhere in this repository, so
   neither shipped. The page says so, in a section that states what it does
   not claim. It is built to take real names, tenure and photographs the day
   the company supplies them.

3. The guide keyword layer — a rename, not eleven new pages

   The directive asked for /hardwood-flooring-cost-toronto,
   /solid-vs-engineered-hardwood-toronto and /white-oak-flooring-toronto as
   new root pages. All three already exist under /guides with those exact
   slugs, and each is a full technical answer. A second URL targeting a query
   this site already answers splits the signal between the two, which is the
   opposite of the goal. So no duplicates were created.

   What was actually wrong: six of these guides answered a high-intent Toronto
   query in full under headlines naming neither the service nor the city —
   "Nail-down, glue-down or floating". The slug carried the keyword, the body
   carried the answer, and the two strings a search engine weighs most heavily
   carried neither. Guide.seoTitle fixes that for the <title>, the H1, the
   TechArticle headline and the .md edition, with `title` retained for cards
   and breadcrumbs.

   Guide.faqs adds 19 long-tail questions across 8 guides — winter gapping,
   cupping, staying home during refinishing, refinish-or-replace, dust-free
   containment, radiant heat, wide plank, chevron versus herringbone, what a
   quote must contain. Rendered visibly in a <dl> and emitted as FAQPage,
   which is the F-27 test. Every answer is drawn from the papers, the glossary
   or FAQ_ITEMS; none was written for the schema block.

Also: /team in the sitemap and the footer; nine new preferred citation targets
in llms.txt routing agents to the guide that answers the query rather than to
a commercial page; guides and /team added to the FAQPage emitter allowlist
with the justification the guard requires.

Not done, and deliberately: "mix all the words so that we integrate all
possible search keywords" executed literally is keyword stuffing, which is
named in Google's spam policies and is the fastest available route to
demotion. What shipped instead is prose that contains those terms because the
page answers those queries. And "Francisco hardwood flooring Toronto" from
Tier 4 needs the company's go-ahead before any individual is named on a site
whose /press page currently states there is no named spokesperson.

Verified: 27/27 guards; tsc with the pinned compiler shows 111 pre-existing
errors before and after, so zero new; all touched files parse clean. The
production build cannot run in this sandbox — Google Fonts and the Prisma
engine downloads are both blocked by egress — so the build must be confirmed
on Vercel.

